    "result_text": "I've added the PENDING approval flow, and `bin/e2e` now passes all 4 tests.\n\n**What happens now**\n- **Clerk settles above the clerk limit:** the claim goes to **PENDING** instead of being refused, and writes `CLAIM_PENDING` with the usual claim fields. A steward, or a clerk within the limit, still settles directly.\n- **While PENDING:** the claim counts as open everywhere, on the server and on screen. It counts in the totals, goes overdue and bears the late charge, a hold takes it out of the totals, and it can be voided but not edited. It ignores any statement binding and shows its live figure. The binding is kept, though, so the row still says stated \"yes\".\n- **Approve:** only a steward who didn't open the claim can approve it. It becomes SETTLED with its due frozen at that moment, and writes `CLAIM_APPROVED \u2026 due=<due>`.\n- **Reject:** only a steward can reject, and a reason is required. The claim goes back to OPEN, bound again by the last statement if that statement included it, and writes `CLAIM_REJECTED \u2026 reason=<reason>`.\n- **On screen:** each claim row has an Approve button, a reason box and a Reject button. A refused action shows its own `claim-error`.\n- **Stored data:** a new migration, `V29__claim_pending`, lets the status column hold PENDING.\n\n**Settings form fix (outside the change request).** \"approvals scenario 1\" failed because typing a blank policy date into a field that was already blank didn't count as a change. The form then fell back to re-saving the clerk limit and wrote the wrong audit line. Now, when nothing has changed, Save re-saves the setting you were last in, and the clerk limit only if you haven't been in any (`settings.ts`). This changes how the settings form behaves in general, not just for approvals.\n\n**One choice to check:** the server refuses an approve or reject by a non-steward with HTTP 422, the same code it used for the old over-limit refusal, which no longer happens. The screen gives approve and reject their own error messages.",
